WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.920
Hey Mahmoud, can you help me read
this Arabic message I got?

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.540
I can't recognise any of the words

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.300
Oh, that's not Arabic.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.240
 

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.780
Hello everyone. Welcome to the LangFocus channel. 
My name is Paul.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.920
In a recent video I talked about the Persian language, 
which is spoken in Iran, Afghanistan, and Tajikistan.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.140
Because Iran is located in the Middle East next to Iraq,

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.140
a lot of people wonder if the Persian
language and Arabic are related.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:30.220
Well, they are not directly related, 
but they have influenced each other.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:35.600
Persian and Arabic belong to completely different language
families, which means that they have separate origins.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:39.690
Arabic is a Semitic language, and it
comes from the same root language as

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:43.230
Hebrew, Aramaic, Phoenician, Ugaritic, etc.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:48.120
Persian, on the other hand, is an Indo-European language 
that shares common roots with the languages

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:53.140
of Northern India, as well as most of the languages 
in Europe, such as English, French, and German.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:58.520
Therefore Persian and Arabic have very different grammar, 
and the way that words are constructed is completely different.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.380
Semitic languages have a unique system 
of mostly three-letter roots,

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.160
and these three-letter roots are put into templates
that form the shape of different words.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.080
For example, the Arabic root for "learn", which 
in English is like DRS.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:18.420
If you want to say, "I learned", you take those three root 
letters and you add them into this template here, 
and say, "asruus".

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.180
Persian does not have this system 
of putting the root into templates.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:26.940
The root words stay in one piece 
and affixes are attached to it.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.960
Let's look at an example in Persian
using the root word "dān", which means "know".

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.280
The first word is "dānestan", which means "to know",

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.720
so if we add the affix, "estan", 
that creates the infinitive of the verb.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:46.160
The next word is "dāneŝ", which means
"knowledge", so the affix "eŝ" creates an abstract noun.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:50.800
Then if we take that word, 
and we add another affix at the end, "dāneŝmand",

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.180
that means scientist, so that creates a concrete noun.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.420
So you can see that nothing is changing 
with the root word itself.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.140
The root word "dān" doesn't change, the letters 
don't get separated the way they would in Arabic.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.620
There are a couple other differences that I think show, that Persian has simpler than Arabic.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:13.000
One of the differences is Persian language 
only has two grammatical cases, 
and only one of them is actually marked,

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.400
whereas Arabic has three grammatical
cases and all three of them are marked.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.820
And other differences that Persian
doesn't have any grammatical gender,

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:25.500
there's no feminine, masculine, or neuter
nouns which is quite a relief to some of us.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:31.060
So, the origins of Arabic and Persian are unrelated, and 
structurally they are completely different languages.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.280
There are two main similarities between
Persian and Arabic: the script, and the vocabulary.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.960
Except for the Persian that is spoken in
Tajikistan, which is written in the Cyrillic script,

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.420
Persian is written in a modified form of the Arabic script.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.020
So what is modified about it?

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.600
Well, in the Persian language, they have some sounds 
that you can't find in the Arabic language.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.000
So they needed to create some new letters
that represent those sounds.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.260
There are four of these new letters
in the Persian version of the Arabic script.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:02.900
They actually come from Arabic letters,
but the markings above or below the letters are different.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:06.640
In Arabic, there is no "p" sound but in Persian there is a "p" sound,

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.780
So they created this new letter 
to represent the "p" sound.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.540
They took the Arabic letter "ب" for "/b/" ["buh"],  the "b" sound,

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:15.100
and they added two more dots underneath that letter.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.260
So when we see that, we know it's a "/p/" ["puh"].

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.060
And in Standard Arabic, there is no "/t͡ʃ/" ["ch"] sound.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.880
So they took the Arabic letter "ج" for ["djuh"], 
and they added two more dots under the letter.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.080
That represents "ch".

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:33.100
There is also no "/ʒə/" ["juh"] sound in Standard Arabic, 
so they took the Arabic letter "ز" for  ["zuh"] , the "Z" sound,

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.780
and they added two more dots above the letter and that makes a "/ʒə/" ["juh"], like the french "J".

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:44.360
Arabic also has no letter for "/g/", the hard G sound, 
so Persian took the letter "ک" for ["kuh"], the "K" sound,

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:48.260
and they added an extra line above it, 
and that represents "/g/", the hard G sound.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:51.480
And some of the other letters represent 
slightly different sounds, too.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:56.060
For example, the letter "waw" in Arabic
becomes "vav" in...uh... Persian.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:56.680
*audible chuckling*

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.520
I almost said Hebrew there because
the letter is also called "vav" in modern Hebrew.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.180
Because of the use of this modified Arabic script,

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.360
people who can't read it just take one look 
at it and because it looks the same,

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.160
they think that it's Arabic 
or they think that it's the same as Arabic,

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:13.440
but anyone who is able to read the script, 
or even just know the letters,

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.460
they'll quickly realise that this is not Arabic,
because you see letters that you don't find in Arabic,

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:20.260
And also, of course, the words are different.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:21.580
 

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.440
Probably not all of the words are different.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:26.480
That's because Persian has
 a  whole lot of Arabic loanwords in it,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.720
that stretch all the way back 
to the Islamic conquest of Persia.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:34.900
In literary Persian, as much as 40%
of the vocabulary is of Arabic origin,

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:38.900
but in colloquial speech the percentage 
of Arabic words is much lower.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:42.790
So even though lots of words have been
adopted from Arabic into Persian,

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:45.720
the structure of those arabic words
has not been adopted.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.380
They're simply adopted as vocabulary as they are.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:51.100
And then they are treated as Persian words 
once they're adopted.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:54.680
Basically, the words that are borrowed
are not broken down into the roots,

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:56.740
which is how they would be treated in Arabic.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:02.880
And the loan words have also adapted 
to the phonology of the Persian language. 
So now they often sound quite different.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:09.260
Here are some example loanwords:
"good morning" in Arabic is "SabaH al-khayr",
good morning in Persian is "Sobh bekheir".

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:13.240
Hello in Persian is "Salaam" [/sɜläm/] which comes
from "Salaam" [/sɜle̞m/] in Arabic

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.680
So you notice that the second "a" sounds a little bit like an "o" in English kind of like "Shalom" [/ʃɜlo̞m/], actually.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:24.080
The Arabic word for "name" is "Ism".
In Persian, it's "Esm". So, just the first vowel is different.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:28.180
In Arabic, the word for "travel" is "śafar". 
In Persian, it's "safár".

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.780
In Arabic, the word for "teaching" is "ta'ayin'liim". 
In Persian, it's "ta'aliim".

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:37.720
So you'll notice that the "ayin" sound from Arabic kind 
of disappears and becomes a simple glottal stop.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.600
The Arabic word for "without" is "biduun".
In Persian, it's "beduuneh".

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:47.620
The Arabic word for "history" is "taariikh", 
the Persian word is "tariikh".

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:50.200
So, just the first "a" vowel seems shorter.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:54.200
The Arabic word for first is "awwal",
but in Persian, it's "avval"

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:57.440
So there we have the "waw" becoming
pronounced like a "vav".

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:02.040
There are also Persian loanwords that
have entered the Arabic language, though not as many.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:07.660
There are some in Standard Arabic but there are more in 
some of the Arabic dialects that are located near Iran,

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.560
Especially in the Iraqi Arabic.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:14.240
But, Persian words in Arabic are harder to
identify because when they're borrowed into Arabic,

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:20.240
they're taken apart and their root is placed into 
Arabic templates to make new Arab-ised words.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:23.160
 

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:26.620
Persian and Arabic are different
languages that are not mutually intelligible,

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:30.040
but my understanding is that 
native speakers hear the other language,

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:32.780
and they recognise quite a few 
of the words that they hear,

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:35.000
but they don't get the overall message.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:38.740
Is it easy for a Persian speaker 
to learn Arabic and vice versa?

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.380
Well, the large amount of shared
vocabulary will definitely help,

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:48.740
in the same way that an English speaker 
learning French has an automatic advantage 
because of all the shared vocabulary.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.680
That is especially helpful for receptive skills.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:57.520
Of course, you still have the challenge of 
learning how to produce correct sentences 
in the grammar of the other language.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.700
Thank you for watching the Langfocus Channel, 
I hope that was helpful.

